QUEUES(2)                      QUEUES FUNCTIONS                       QUEUES(2)NAME       queues - double linked list definitionsSYNOPSIS       #include queues.f83       queues ( -- )       QUEUE ( -- )       +succ ( queue -- succ) private       +pred ( queue -- pred) private       succ ( queue -- succ)       pred ( queue -- pred)       ?empty ( queue -- boolean)       into ( item queue -- )       out ( item -- )       empty ( queue -- )       length ( queue -- length)       ?member ( item queue -- flag)       map ( queue block[item -- ] -- )       ?map ( queue block[item -- flag] -- )       print ( queue -- )       .queue ( queue -- )DESCRIPTION       Kernel support word set for double linked circular lists.queues ( -- )       Vocabulary containing double linked circular list definitions.QUEUE ( -- )       Structure definition of double linked list. Used as follows to:               new QUEUE       Creates a new queue item. An unnamed instance for a queue header.               QUEUE <queue-name>       Creates a named queue item.+succ ( queue -- succ) private       Modifiers queue pointer to access successor pointer in queue       structure. A ptr field name.+pred ( queue -- pred) private       Modifiers queue pointer to access predecessor pointer in queue       structure. A ptr field name.succ ( queue -- succ)        Returns pointer to successor queue item.pred ( queue -- pred)       Returns pointer to predecessor queue item.?empty ( queue -- boolean)       Checks if the queue is empty. Successor pointer points to itself.into ( item queue -- )       Inserts item into queue as new predecessor element to queue item. If       the queue parameter is a queue header the item is inserted last        into the queue.out ( item -- )       Removes an item for any queue. Observe this function does not        require knowledge about which queue the item is in.map ( queue block[item -- ] -- )       Used as following in either execution or compilation mode:               <queue> <block> map       Performs the parameterized block on each item in the queue. ?map ( queue block[item -- flag] -- )       Used as following in either execution or compilation mode:               <queue> <conditional-block> ?map       Performs the parameterized block on each item in the queue        until the block returns true.   print ( queue -- )       Prints the address of each item in a queue.length ( queue -- length)       Returns the length of a queue. The queue head is counted. Returns a       integer larger than zero.?member ( item queue -- flag)       Search for the item in the given queue. If found returns true else       false. The item may not be nil as this will give an incorrect        result..queue ( queue -- )       Prints information about a queue.SEE ALSO       forth(1), structures(2), blocks(2)WARNING       Code written using this word set is not directly portable to       other forth environments.BUG       Pointers are not check. Illegal pointer value will result in       a possible error situation.     COPYING       Copyright (C) 1989 Mikael R.K. Patel       Permission is granted to make and distribute verbatim copies       of this manual provided the copyright notice and this permission       notice are preserved on all copies.       Permission is granted to copy and distribute modified versions       of this manual under the conditions for verbatim copying,        provided also that the section entitled "GNU General Public       License" is included exactly as in the original, and provided       that the entire resulting derived work is distributed under       the terms of a permission notice identical to this one.       Permission is granted to copy and distribute translations of       this manual into another language, under the above conditions       for modified versions, except that the section entitled "GNU       Gener